From 8e6534e7ad606bf6b9709773b0a5a68dc6a031ff Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Bloomin <452472+daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 09:01:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(serve): SSH runtime markers no longer read as PM drift,
 and runtime retirement can't loop [daveey]

An SSH-isolated serve writes .hermes-ssh-runtime-<nonce> into its interpreter's site-packages. On a
host whose serve runs the store Python, that is PM's published python tree, and tree_digest hashed
the marker. Every scripts/run_tests.sh (setup-hermes.sh --runtime-only -> pm install
--trust-recorded -> check_project_lock -> _toolchain's ensure("uv", explicit) re-hash) then saw the
python entry as drifted and re-realized it, deleting every marker; since 19c6caabe0 each SSH backend
then retired, and the always-on dashboard restarted every ~70 s on the busy hub.

- pm.store.tree_digest skips files named exactly .hermes-ssh-runtime-<16 hex> (process state, like
  __pycache__), so an intact runtime holding markers is never repaired away.
- The serve records a content fingerprint of its purelib at boot (dist-info RECORDs, top-level
  files, pyvenv.cfg, interpreter). On a lost marker over identical bytes it writes the marker back
  and stays up (runtimeIntact true again) instead of retiring.
- A real runtime retirement is persisted per owner nonce; another one within 15 min is held, so one
  bad signal can't restart a backend every minute. Code-revision retirement is unchanged, and every
  retirement still goes through the idle fence between turns.
